<h2>The case before the court</h2>
<p>The applicant, a practising advocate, sought anticipatory bail in an FIR registered under provisions of the Bharatiya Nyaya Sanhita, 2023 arising from an incident in which a woman was allegedly molested and her husband and son assaulted and disrobed. The incident was captured on CCTV, and the footage — described by the court as shocking and barbaric — formed part of the investigative record. While the application was pending, the trial court declared the applicant a proclaimed offender on 12 October 2026 for evading process.</p>
<p>Before Justice Girish Kathpalia, the applicant advanced three principal contentions: that the proclamation could not have been made while his bail application was pending; that the allegations did not warrant custodial interrogation; and that his standing as an advocate told in his favour. Each failed.</p>

<h2>Proclamation and the shrunken doorway</h2>
<p>The structural point first. A proclamation issues only after a court is satisfied that the accused is absconding or concealing himself so that a warrant cannot be executed. An accused who then seeks anticipatory bail is, in substance, asking the system to protect him from the very process he has defeated. The Supreme Court has therefore confined pre-arrest bail for proclaimed persons to exceptional cases, and the Delhi High Court applied exactly that standard: anticipatory bail is not absolutely barred, but it is available only where something exceptional appears — such as a prima facie finding that no case is made out. On this record, with graphic CCTV footage and an unrecovered DVR, no such exception existed.</p>
<div class="note">
<p>The court also dispatched the pendency argument. No stay operated on the proclamation proceedings, and the mere filing of a bail application is not an appearance before the court nor an answer to evasion. An accused cannot park an application in one forum and treat it as immunity in another.</p>
</div>

<h2>The factors that decided the application</h2>
<div class="flow">
<div class="fstep"><strong>Conduct of the accused.</strong> Evasion of process culminating in a proclaimed offender declaration on 12 October 2026 — conduct that itself disentitles an applicant seeking the discretion of the court.</div>
<div class="fstep"><strong>Nature of the allegations.</strong> CCTV footage of the assault that the court found shocking and barbaric; anticipatory bail jurisprudence has always weighed the gravity and character of the offence.</div>
<div class="fstep"><strong>Needs of the investigation.</strong> A removed digital video recorder yet to be recovered, making custodial interrogation a genuine investigative requirement rather than a formal incantation.</div>
<div class="fstep"><strong>Status of the applicant.</strong> Dismissed as irrelevant: being an advocate, the court held, is no licence for lawlessness and adds nothing to the merits of a bail plea.</div>
</div>

<h2>Where this fits in the law of pre-arrest bail</h2>
<p>Anticipatory bail under Section 482 of the Bharatiya Nagarik Suraksha Sanhita, 2023 — the successor to Section 438 CrPC — protects personal liberty against arrest on frivolous or motivated accusations. But the remedy presumes an applicant who engages with the process. Once proclamation intervenes, the presumption inverts: the applicant must first explain the evasion, and only an exceptional case — typically one where the accusation itself collapses on its face — will earn protection. Decisions of the Supreme Court on absconders and proclaimed offenders have repeatedly made the point, and this order carries it forward into BNS-era practice in Delhi.</p>
<p>Equally notable is the treatment of custodial interrogation in the order. Courts deciding pre-arrest bail must weigh whether the investigation genuinely requires the accused in custody — here, the unrecovered DVR supplied a concrete, articulable need, illustrating the difference between a recovery-based justification and a routine assertion.</p>

<h2>Practical guidance</h2>
<p>For accused persons, the lesson is blunt: respond to summons and warrants, appear through counsel and in person where required, and never allow proclamation proceedings to mature while a bail application pends — the declaration transforms the legal landscape and very nearly forecloses pre-arrest relief. An accused already proclaimed should ordinarily surrender, apply for regular bail, and challenge any defect in the proclamation separately rather than press anticipatory bail on merits alone. For complainants, the order shows how evasion and outstanding recoveries can be marshalled to oppose protection. And for professionals of every stripe, it is a reminder that the courts treat status as neutral: the robe neither aggravates nor excuses.</p>
